.TH EZFLATPAK 1 "2026-09-20" "caveutils" "caveutils Manual"
.SH NAME
flatseal \- terminal manager for Flatpak apps and their permissions, in
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B flatseal apps
.br
.B flatseal perms APP
.br
.B flatseal global
.br
.B flatseal audit [--plain]
.br
.B flatseal who KEY [--plain]
.br
.B flatseal allow|deny|default APP KEY...
.br
.B flatseal show APP [modified]
.br
.B flatseal export [FILE] | import FILE
.br
.B flatseal ENTER
.br
.B flatseal CTRL-G
.br
.B flatseal CTRL-X
.br
.B flatseal CTRL-U
.br
.B flatseal CTRL-N
.br
.B flatseal CTRL-Z
.br
.B flatseal ALT-Z
.br
.B flatseal ALT-M
.br
.B flatseal ALT-G
.br
.B flatseal ALT-A
.br
.B flatseal ALT-T
.br
.B flatseal ALT-P
.br
.B flatseal ALT-B
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B flatseal
.nf
one tool, built on lib/lazytui.sh. It wraps the real flatpak, which it
finds in PATH skipping itself.

  flatseal [apps | perms APP | global | APP-ID]

Apps (the default view) lists what is installed. Arrows or Tab move,
space marks rows like nnn, / searches, and update / uninstall act on all
marked rows. Enter opens the permissions of the current app. The first row,
"All applications", is the global layer (like Flatseal): Enter opens the global
overrides; Uninstall / Update / Run do nothing useful on it.

Permissions is Flatseal in the terminal. Every permission the app really
has is listed with its effective state, layered the way flatpak layers
them: the app's manifest, then your global overrides, then your overrides
for that app:

  on   allowed        off   denied
  *    set by an override of this app      g   set by a global override

Rows cover sockets, devices, shares, features, filesystems, persistent
dirs, environment variables and the D-Bus names, plus the usual
suspects the app doesn't mention. Broad ones that are on show in red.
Space marks rows; Enter toggles every marked row (or the current one).
Allow, deny and "default" (drop your override) all work on the layer you
are in, so denying something globally and re-allowing it for one app both
work - the two things Flatseal's users asked for most.

Global is the same list for the "all applications" layer, i.e. plain
`flatpak override --user`. Everything is a per-user override, exactly
what Flatseal writes, and no manifest is ever touched.

Beyond what Flatseal does:
  Portals    notifications, background, microphone, speakers, camera and location are rows too
             (the xdg-desktop-portal permission store); they can be set any time, not only once the
             portal has stored something for the app.
  Audit      ALT-A: every app with a risk score (whole filesystem, session/system bus, all devices,
             sandbox escape via org.freedesktop.Flatpak, ...), riskiest first. CTRL-W: which apps have
             a given permission. Also `flatseal audit --plain` and `flatseal who KEY --plain`.
  Try once   ALT-T: run the app with the marked rows flipped, without saving anything.
  Bulk       ALT-P (apps list): allow / deny / drop one permission for all marked apps.
  Version    permissions the installed flatpak is too old to know are not listed.
  Scripts    allow / deny / default / show / export / import work without any UI.

The lists refresh by themselves every REFRESH_MS milliseconds (top of
the script, or FLATPAK_REFRESH; 0 turns it off).

            ~/.local/state/flatpak-tui/backups
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B apps
installed apps (default)
.TP
.B perms APP
permissions of an app (APP-ID alone works too)
.TP
.B global
permissions of all apps at once
.TP
.B ENTER
apps: open permissions, permissions: toggle marked rows
.TP
.B CTRL-G
allow the marked rows
.TP
.B CTRL-X
deny the marked rows
.TP
.B CTRL-U
drop your override of the marked rows
.TP
.B CTRL-N
add a permission that isn't listed
.TP
.B CTRL-Z
reset every override of this layer (asks first)
.TP
.B ALT-Z
undo the last change to this layer; up to 20 versions are kept in
.TP
.B ALT-M
show only what is overridden
.TP
.B ALT-G
switch to the global layer
.TP
.B ALT-A
audit: risk score of every app; CTRL-W there: who has a permission
.TP
.B ALT-T
run the app once with the marked rows flipped (not saved)
.TP
.B ALT-P
apps: change one permission for all marked apps
.TP
.B ALT-B
back to the app list
